--- What was actually observed, and what was NOT run ---
Two un-nested (single-level) sandbox-exec calls were issued from inside this session's own
Claude Code Bash: one applying outer-deny-read.sb directly (n=1), one applying
inner-permissive.sb alone (n=2). Both exited 71 with "sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted".
No nested call (one sandbox-exec launching a second) was ever issued for either arm (a) or arm
(b) -- both are NOT RUN on this host, not concluded. Whether the exit-71 result is caused
specifically by Claude Code's own enclosing Seatbelt profile, or by sandbox_apply failing more
generally on this host, was not established here; see 12-SPIKE-RESULTS.md S10 for the rerun plan
that would settle it.

--- What remains unresolved ---
Whether this EPERM is specific to Claude Code's OWN enclosing Seatbelt profile (i.e., an
unsandboxed process, or a process sandboxed some other way, CAN successfully call sandbox_apply
a second time) was not established this session. The comparison run (identical command with
dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true) was itself refused by the auto-mode classifier ("Safety Bypass
Flag") before it could execute. That comparison is the one piece of first-hand evidence that
would either confirm "Claude's own Seatbelt specifically forbids re-sandboxing" or "sandbox_apply
is broken/entitlement-gated on this host regardless of Claude" -- and it needs a human (not an
agent, per the classifier's own denial) to run it, or an explicit founder-approved Bash permission
rule for sandbox-exec.
